To understand Endpoints in Kubernetes, you have to understand the fundamental problem they solve: Pods are ephemeral, but Services are stable.

When a Pod dies or scales out, its IP address changes or new IPs appear. Clients shouldn't have to keep track of these dynamic IPs. That is where Services and Endpoints work together.

The Core Relationship: Service → Endpoints → Pods

A Kubernetes Service is essentially just an abstraction—a stable virtual IP (ClusterIP) and a DNS name. The Service itself does not handle or route traffic directly.Instead, Kubernetes automatically creates an Endpoints object behind the scenes with the exact same name as your Service:

+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                     Service: web-service                    |
|                 ClusterIP: 10.96.142.20:80                  |
|                 Selector: app=web-app                       |
+------------------------------+------------------------------+
                               |
                               v  (managed automatically)
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|                    Endpoints: web-service                   |
|       Subsets / Addresses:                                  |
|         - 10.244.0.5:80  (Points to Pod: web-app-xxxx-1)    |
|         - 10.244.0.6:80  (Points to Pod: web-app-xxxx-2)    |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

How it connects:
When you define selector: app=web-app on the Service, the Endpoint Controller (inside kube-controller-manager) continuously watches for Pods matching that label.

If a Pod is healthy (passes readiness probes) and matches the label, the controller grabs its dynamic Pod IP and writes it into the web-service Endpoints list.

If a Pod crashes or terminates, the controller immediately removes its IP from the Endpoints list.

2. How Traffic Actually Flows (kube-proxy)
The Endpoints resource is the bridge between Kubernetes API resources and your node’s actual networking layer:

Incoming Request to 10.96.142.20:80 (ClusterIP)
                               |
                               v
                     [ Node Kernel: iptables / IPVS ]
                     (Programmed by kube-proxy using Endpoints data)
                               |
                 +-------------+-------------+
                 | (Load balanced 50/50)     |
                 v                           v
         Pod 1 (10.244.0.5:80)       Pod 2 (10.244.0.6:80)


kube-proxy watches the API server for changes to Services and their Endpoints.

When the Endpoints list updates, kube-proxy configures low-level Linux networking rules (iptables or IPVS) on every single worker node.

When traffic hits the Service IP (ClusterIP), the Linux kernel translates that virtual IP into one of the actual Pod IPs listed in the Endpoints object using random load balancing.

3. Why the Deprecation Warning? (Endpoints vs. EndpointSlice)
You saw this warning earlier:

Warning: v1 Endpoints is deprecated in v1.33+; use discovery.k8s.io/v1 EndpointSlice

Here is the architectural reason for this change:

The Old v1.Endpoints Limitation (Monolithic):

All Pod IPs for a service were stored inside a single object.

If a deployment scaled to 5,000 Pods, that single Endpoints object became massive (megabytes of data).

If just one Pod restarted, the entire object had to be updated and transmitted over the network to kube-proxy on every node, creating high CPU and network spikes.

The Modern EndpointSlice Solution (Chunked):

Rather than one giant list, Kubernetes slices backend IPs into smaller groups (by default, up to 100 endpoints per slice).

If a Pod restarts, only a single slice (holding ~100 IPs) updates, reducing control-plane traffic and memory overhead by orders of magnitude.

You can inspect your cluster's modern EndpointSlice right now:

kubectl get endpointslice -l kubernetes.io/service-name=web-service -o yaml

4. Advanced DevOps Concept: Services Without Selectors (Custom Endpoints)
In production or technical interviews, you may encounter scenarios where you need a Kubernetes Service to point to an external database or third-party API outside the cluster (e.g., an AWS RDS instance or an on-prem Oracle DB).

You do this by creating a Service without a selector, and manually creating your own Endpoints object:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: external-database
spec:
  ports:
    - port: 5432
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Endpoints
metadata:
  name: external-database   # Must match the Service name exactly!
subsets:
  - addresses:
      - ip: 192.168.1.50     # External IP of your physical DB server
    ports:
      - port: 5432


Now, any Pod inside the cluster can simply connect to http://external-database:5432, completely abstracting away the external infrastructure!



